rong ROC-AUC (~0.86). Validation plan: fit on the 120k train split only, impute MonthlyIncome/NumberOfDependents medians computed on train, then LightGBM binary logloss, 5-fold-free single held-out early stopping (use the provided validation features only for early stopping, never its labels — instead use an internal split of the 120k train rows for early stopping). Report accuracy on probability, not thresholded labels. Also audit: missingness rates, sentinel values (96/98 in delinquency columns), extreme RevolvingUtilization>1, DebtRatio outliers, duplicate feature rows, and base rate 6.684%.\n- Load train_data/train.csv and predict_data/test.csv; assert column set, dtypes, row counts (120000/101503) and ID alignment for prediction output order.\n- Print missing value counts, describe() quantiles for every feature, count of delinquency-column values in {96,98}, count of RevolvingUtilization>1, and target rate — record as baseline data-quality report.\n- Baseline model: median impute MonthlyIncome, NumberOfDependents; LightGBM (num_leaves=31, learning_rate=0.05, n_estimators=600, early stopping on 15% internal train split, seed=42, n_jobs=4); save validation Id,Probability and predictions.csv.\n- Compute and log validation ROC-AUC plus feature importance, store model + imputer pipeline and a run log artifact.\n- Controlled feature engineering with domain-motivated transforms. Hypothesis: this dataset has heavy tails and sentinel encodings; capped/ratio features and a "ever-delinquent" flag will lift AUC over the raw baseline by 0.002–0.005. Plan: keep the same preprocessing fit-on-train discipline and the same LightGBM hyperparameters as the baseline so the only change is features; compare measured validation ROC-AUC on the identical 30k Id set.\n- Cap RevolvingUtilizationOfUnsecuredLines at 1.0 and flag values >1; add log1p of DebtRatio, MonthlyIncome and RevolvingUtilization where non-negative.\n- Create delinquency aggregates: total past-due count = 30-59 + 60-89 + 90+; binary flags for any 30-59/60-89/90+ lateness; treat {96,98} as "high lateness" bucket mapped to a distinct high count.\n- Add MonthlyIncomeImputed flag, income-per-person (MonthlyIncome / (NumberOfDependents+1)), and DebtRatio capped at a train-quantile (e.g. 0.995) bound.\n- Retrain the identical LightGBM and log AUC delta vs baseline; if AUC drops, keep the baseline and record the failed hypothesis.\n- Ensemble/stability path: average LightGBM with a shallow high-bias variant plus a logistic/HistGradientBoosting model on standardized features. Hypothesis: this dataset\'s AUC is mostly bias-limited and a small ensemble reduces variance on the fixed validation set, giving a modest but real gain (~0.001–0.003). Cost: ~2–3× baseline runtime, still CPU-only, ~2GB RAM.\n- Train HistGradientBoostingClassifier (sklearn, seed=42) on median-imputed, quantile-clipped features; log AUC.\n- Train a LightGBM variant with num_leaves=15, feature_fraction=0.8, more estimators for regularization; log AUC.\n- Blend by rank-average (0.5/0.3/0.2 weights tuned only on the 120k train via internal split, not on the fixed validation labels more than once) and report the single measured validation AUC.\n- Persist the chosen winner model + preprocessing and regenerate validation and prediction CSVs from it only.\n- Error analysis and calibration check before finalizing. Hypothesis: at very low predicted probability the model may systematically under-separate borderline defaulters; calibration inspection will reveal whether further feature work is warranted or whether we should stop at the best measured AUC. Plan: bucket validation predictions into deciles, compare mean predicted probability vs. observed positive rate (using the private-scored AUC only through the evaluator return, not by reading hidden labels ourselves), and examine false negatives among high-confidence band. Do not ship threshold tuning — the metric needs probabilities.\n- Produce reliability-style decile table on train-side out-of-fold predictions (internal split) since hidden validation labels are unavailable.\n- Inspect misranked high-risk cases: compare feature distributions of false negatives vs true positives in internal split to spot missing features.\n- Confirm predictions.csv has exactly 101503 rows in test.csv Id order, finite [0,1] probabilities, no index column; confirm validation CSV has 30000 rows aligned to validation Ids.\n- Save artifacts: model binary, preprocessing object, measured validation ROC-AUC, and a short inference README; return both file paths.</ideas>\n<local_experiments>\n<failed_attempts></failed_attempts></local_experiments>\nThe business requirements define the target, primary metric, validation rules,\npermitted inputs and output schema. Use only supplied local data and completed\nlocal experiments. Do not download datasets, pretrained models or predictions.\nStart with a simple reproducible baseline. Keep the evaluator\'s split fixed,\nfit preprocessing on training rows only, and never read hidden validation labels.\nEvery improvement needs a testable hypothesis and comparable validation evidence.\nTreat failed and unscored attempts as lessons, not successful results.\nUse CPU when a library lacks support for the selected accelerator.\n\nMerge duplicate ideas and remove disproven or infeasible changes. Preserve useful\ntechnical details and business requirements. Rank actions by measured weaknesses,\nexpected benefit and cost. Keep at most 20 actionable ideas; preserve a baseline\nuntil a valid local result exists. Respond with repeated <idea>...</idea> tags.\n'}]
