n- Logistic/linear reference model. Fit a scaled, median-imputed logistic regression on the engineered features as a low-variance reference and diversity candidate. Do not expect it to beat LightGBM; use it to (a) confirm the tree model\'s lift is real and (b) potentially contribute a small weight to the ensemble. Log its measured validation ROC-AUC.\n- Internal out-of-fold error analysis and calibration check. Using internal 120k train splits only (never hidden labels), produce a decile table of mean predicted probability vs observed positive rate and inspect misranked high-risk cases (false negatives vs true positives feature distributions) to see whether any missing-signal feature is implied. Ship probabilities only — no threshold tuning, since the metric is ROC-AUC over probabilities. Record findings as guidance for whether further feature work is justified.\n- Finalize winner and artifacts. Pick the candidate with the highest measured fixed-validation ROC-AUC (default to the champion if none beat it). Persist that model + its preprocessing pipeline, write validation Id,Probability (30000 rows), predictions.csv (101503 rows, test.csv Id order, two columns, no index), and an inference README documenting the measured ROC-AUC, feature set, hyperparameters, seed=42, and the exact pipeline used to generate both files. Return both file paths.</ideas>\n<local_experiments>\n<experiment>\n<id>round-001-experiment-01</id>\n<title>LGBM Baseline + Audit</title>\n<description>**Hypothesis.** A CPU LightGBM binary classifier with domain-aware feature engineering, median imputation, and multi-seed bagging establishes a strong, reproducible ROC-AUC baseline for predicting SeriousDlqin2yrs=1, materially above chance.\n\n**Best measured version.** The highest fixed-split validation score achieved was **0.8705** (from the 33-feature 5-seed bag of the tuned `num_leaves=15, lr=0.03, reg_lambda=1.0, min_child_samples=40, subsample=0.9, colsample_bytree=0.8` config, `n_estimators≈371` chosen by internal early stopping). Note: its internal holdout AUC was 0.86825 — slightly *below* the 3-way rank blend that measured 0.86872 on holdout — yet it scored best on the fixed split, illustrating holdout/fixed-split drift at this tight margin (0.868–0.871 range). The engine-recorded best is 0.8705, so that is reported as champion.\n\n**Processing.**\n- Median imputation of `MonthlyIncome` (5390) and `NumberOfDependents` (0), fit on the 120k training rows only.\n- Sentinel codes 96/98 in the three delinquency columns capped at 20; captured by a `delinq_sentinel` flag.\n- Revolving utilization clipped to [0, 1.5] plus a `ru_gt1_flag`.\n- Engineered features: missing flags, `total_delinq`, `delinq_weighted`, `ru_x_delinq`, `debt_per_income`, `income_per_dependent/line`, `log_MonthlyIncome/DebtRatio`, `debt_per_line`, `has_delinq_any`, `age_x_delinq`, `re_loan_ratio`, `util_x_dti`, `debt_pressure`, `ru_over_lines`, `sev_delinq_rate` (33 total features; Id excluded).\n\n**Validation strategy.** Fixed 30000-row validation never used for fitting or early stopping; internal 15% stratified holdout on the 120k train drives `n_estimators` and config. Seed = 42 global; bag seeds [42,7,123,2024,555]; `n_jobs=4`; CPU only.\n\n**Evidence (executed).**\n- Baseline single LGBM (leaves=31, lr=0.05, 19 raw feats): holdout AUC 0.86674 → fixed 0.8680.\n- FE single model: holdout 0.86683 → fixed 0.8685.\n- LGBM+LR rank blend (19 feats): holdout 0.86688 → fixed 0.8693.\n- Tuned leaves=15/lr=0.03 4-seed bag: holdout 0.86856 → fixed 0.8700.\n- 28-feature 4-seed bag: holdout 0.86840 → fixed 0.8702.\n- 3-way rank blend (19bag+28bag+LR, w≈0.5/0.45/0.05): holdout 0.86872 → fixed 0.8703.\n- **33-feature 5-seed bag: holdout 0.86825 → fixed 0.8705 (best).**\n\n**Changes from parent.** Parent was None (first round); established the initial baseline and iterated through feature engineering, model regularization, LR blending, and bagging.\n\n**Artifacts** (working dir): `predictions.csv` (101503 rows, test Id order, Id,Probability), `validation_predictions.csv` (30000 rows), `artifacts/lgbm_bag33_*.txt` (5 boosters, one per seed), `artifacts/run_meta_bag33.json` (features, medians, n_it, seeds, holdout AUC), `artifacts/run_log.txt` (all rounds).\n\n**Load the saved model:** reconstruct features via the `build()` function using the stored `features` and `medians` from `run_meta_bag33.json`, then for each booster `lgb.Booster(model_file=\'artifacts/lgbm_bag33_<seed>.txt\')` and average `predict()` positive probabilities across the 5 seeds.</description>\n<code># Concise outline of best measured approach (33-feature, 5-seed LightGBM bag)\n1. Read train/test/validation CSV from ../input/data.\n2. build(df): median-impute MonthlyIncome & NumberOfDependents (medians from train);\n   cap delinquency sentinels 96/98 -> 20 with flag; clip utilization to 1.5 with gt1 flag;\n   add 33 engineered features (missing flags, ratios, log, delinq aggregates).\n3. y = SeriousDlqin2yrs; internal split train_test_split(test_size=0.15, stratify, seed=42).\n4. Probe LGBM(leaves=15, lr=0.03, reg_lambda=1.0, min_child_samples=40, subsample=0.9,\n   colsample_bytree=0.8) with early_stopping(100) -> n_it ~371.\n5. Bag 5 seeds [42,7,123,2024,555] on 15% holdout -> holdout AUC (~0.868).\n6. Refit the 5-seed bag on full 120k, average predict_proba over seeds for validation & test.\n7. Assert 30000 / 101503 rows, finite probabilities in [0,1], unique Ids.\n8. Write validation_predictions.csv and predictions.csv; save 5 boosters + run_meta JSON.</code>\n<validation_metric>Metric↑(0.8705)</validation_metric>\n<suggestions>**Observed weaknesses / lessons (evidence-based):**\n- Holdout AUC and fixed-split metric diverge at the 0.868–0.871 margin (e.g., 3-way blend had higher holdout 0.86872 but lower fixed 0.8703 than the 33-feat bag whose holdout was 0.86825). Differences of ±0.0003 are within noise — chasing them risks overfitting the fixed split indirectly. Treat gains <0.0005 as inconclusive.\n- Adding raw feature breadth (28→33 features) lowered holdout AUC (0.86840→0.86825) yet raised fixed split (0.8702→0.8705) — feature addition is not monotonically helpful; tree models already capture most interactions.\n- Probability calibration was never checked (only ranking/AUC matters here, so acceptable for the metric, but not for downstream thresholding).\n- Duplicate feature rows (464) and heavy DebtRatio tail remain unaddressed; may add noise.\n\n**Prioritized next experiments (hypotheses to test, not yet measured):**\n1. **Wider seed bag / repeated CV** (e.g., 8–10 seeds) to reduce variance in the final probability — likely small, stable gain; monitor runtime (<300s cell cap).\n2. **Fixed-split vs holdout reconciliation**: use the same stratification ratio and larger internal holdout (20%) to better mirror the fixed split; re-select config by internal metric and cross-check that gains hold. Hypothesis: prior "improvements" partly tracked fixed-split noise.\n3. **CatBoost-free GOSS/DART LightGBM variant** and/or `is_unbalance`/scale_pos_weight — test whether imbalance handling helps AUC (usually minimal for AUC, but worth one controlled run).\n4. **Feature selection**: prune the 33 features to those with top gain importance ≥ threshold to test whether fewer, cleaner features recover the 0.86872 holdout while holding fixed split; risk of regression is moderate.\n5. **Monotonic constraints** on delinquency counts / utilization (credit-risk domain knowledge) — could improve generalization; not yet tested.\n\n**Do not**: rely on holdout deltas below 0.0005 as real signal; the fixed split stays authoritative but measured only by evaluator.</suggestions>\n</experiment>\n\n<failed_attempts></failed_attempts></local_experiments>\nThe business requirements define the target, primary metric, validation rules,\npermitted inputs and output schema. Use only supplied local data and completed\nlocal experiments. Do not download datasets, pretrained models or predictions.\nStart with a simple reproducible baseline. Keep the evaluator\'s split fixed,\nfit preprocessing on training rows only, and never read hidden validation labels.\nEvery improvement needs a testable hypothesis and comparable validation evidence.\nTreat failed and unscored attempts as lessons, not successful results.\nUse CPU when a library lacks support for the selected accelerator.\n\nPropose at least four feasible solution paths. On the first round prioritize a\ncheap baseline and data-quality checks. On later rounds identify weaknesses in\nmeasured local results, avoid repeating failed attempts, and propose controlled\nchanges with a hypothesis, expected evidence, runtime and memory cost.\nInclude error analysis, calibration or interpretability when business goals need them.\nRespond using repeated tags:\n<solution><description>Approach, hypothesis and validation plan</description>\n<idea>One concrete implementation step</idea><idea>Another step</idea></solution>\n'}]
